# Benchmarks

What pg_stat_statement_context costs on top of `pg_stat_statements` alone, measured with pgbench. This page is **generated**: `bench/analyze.py doc` fills in the results from the raw data committed under [`bench/results/`](../bench/results/), and the prose comes from [`bench/benchmarks.md.in`](../bench/benchmarks.md.in). How to run the suite, and how to run it on dedicated hardware, is in [`bench/README.md`](../bench/README.md).

**Read this first.** The only campaign so far ran in a **Docker Desktop VM on a laptop (Apple M1 Max, macOS)**. That is not production hardware: the VM's vCPUs float across performance and efficiency cores, and the host's scheduler, power management and other processes add noise that nothing inside the VM can see. Treat the results as relative costs on that machine, read their confidence intervals, and don't quote the absolute TPS or latency. Dedicated Linux x86 and Graviton runs are left to the maintainers ([instructions](../bench/README.md#dedicated-hardware)); their results will appear here when they are committed.

## Summary

From the newest campaign below (2026-10-09: PG 18.6, release build, Docker on an Apple M1 Max; 10 paired blocks of 15 s runs). Intervals are pointwise 95% confidence intervals; see [What the data can and cannot bound](#what-the-data-can-and-cannot-bound) for how to read them.

**A tagged statement** (sqlcommenter comment, 2 kept tags, default settings) over the twelve core workloads (simple and prepared protocol, read-only and TPC-B-like, 1, N and 4N clients), generated from the data: {{headline}} In the core workloads the extension's latency effect showed up at the median (p50 +2% to +10% where resolved); no p99 interval of a core workload excludes zero, and their upper ends reach +7% to +78%, so the local data does not bound the tail.

| What | Extra server CPU per statement vs `pg_stat_statements` alone | Latency (paired, pointwise) |
|---|---|---|
| An untagged statement (no comment, `untagged = skip`) | +1.0 µs [+0.1, +1.9] (prepared) to +2.4 µs [+1.1, +3.7] (simple); not resolved for TPC-B (+1.5 µs [−0.1, +3.0]) | p50 +3% [+2, +5] (simple); p99 not resolved |
| A `regex` extractor merged with sqlcommenter, plus a `normalize` rule | +7.8 to +9.8 µs | p50 +7% to +15%; p99 not resolved |
| 5,000 other entries in the store; the exporter recipe scraped every 15 s | +3.3 and +3.0 µs (not paired against the defaults) | not resolved |
| The exporter recipe scraped every 1 s | +4.5 µs [+3.6, +5.5] | p50 +4% [+2, +7]; **p99 +19% [+6, +33]** |
| 1 s buckets (a rollover every second) | +4.6 µs [+3.4, +5.9] | p50 +4% [+3, +6]; p99 +14% [−3, +33] not resolved; no latency concentration at bucket boundaries |
| Nested PL/pgSQL, `track = all` | +1.1 µs (`nested_tags = inherit`), +1.8 µs (`scan`) per statement | **p99 +18% [+3.5, +35]** (`inherit`), **+26% [+11, +44]** (`scan`) |
| Nested PL/pgSQL, `track = top` | +0.4 µs per statement (+7 µs per call of a function running 16 statements) | p50 +4% [+3, +6]; p99 not resolved |
| A 59 kB `IN` list (≈ 5.8 ms of server CPU each) | not resolved: `position = any` +0.2 ms [−0.6, +1.0] | not resolved; a residual bias of ~5% between runs hides smaller effects |
| Sustained eviction (a new tag set every statement, `max_entries = 10000`) | +5.6 µs [+4.3, +6.8] | **p99 +60% [+38, +84]**, also under the simultaneous correction |

What this supports: on this host, the extension's cost is a few microseconds of CPU per statement. That matters for sub-millisecond point queries (single-digit percent of their CPU) and is negligible for statements that take milliseconds. Tail latency rises measurably with `track = all`, an aggressive 1 s scrape, and sustained eviction. What it does not support: any statement about other hardware, a bound on the tail latency of the default configuration, or overheads below about 1 µs per statement or 5% of a long statement. Dedicated-hardware runs are needed for those; see [bench/README.md](../bench/README.md#dedicated-hardware).

## Scenarios

Each **scenario** is one pgbench script, protocol and client count. In it, each **configuration** is compared with a baseline that preloads only `pg_stat_statements` and runs the same script. *N* is the number of CPUs the server is pinned to (5 in the local campaign).

| Scenario | What it runs | Configurations (baseline first) |
|---|---|---|
| `ro-{simple,prepared}-c{1,N,4N}` | `SELECT abalance FROM pgbench_accounts WHERE aid = :aid` (the `pgbench -S` statement) with an appended 190-byte sqlcommenter comment (six tags, two of them in the default allowlist), simple or prepared protocol, 1, N or 4N clients | `pgss`; `ext` (this extension, defaults) |
| `rw-{simple,prepared}-c{1,N,4N}` | the pgbench TPC-B-like transaction (`BEGIN`, three `UPDATE`s, a `SELECT`, an `INSERT`, `END`), each of the 5 statements with that comment | `pgss`; `ext` |
| `ro-simple-cN` (extra) | as above | `plain-pgss` and `plain-ext` (the statement **without** a comment: the untagged path, `untagged = skip`); `ext-regex-normalize` (a `regex` extractor merged with sqlcommenter and a `normalize` rule, `/users/42` → `/users/:id`); `ext-store5k` (the store holds 5,000 other entries before the run); `ext-reader15s` and `ext-reader1s` (the [postgres_exporter recipe](integrations/postgres_exporter/)'s three queries every 15 s or every 1 s, each from a new connection, during the run); `ext-1s-buckets` (`bucket_interval = 1s`: a bucket rollover every second) |
| `ro-prepared-cN`, `rw-simple-cN` (extra) | as above | `plain-pgss`, `plain-ext`, `ext-regex-normalize` |
| `nested-simple-cN` | a PL/pgSQL function with two nested loops that runs 16 commented inner `SELECT`s, called from a commented top-level `SELECT` (17 statements per transaction) | `pgss`, `ext` (`track = top`); `pgss-all` (`pg_stat_statements.track = all`), `ext-all-inherit` and `ext-all-scan` (also this extension's `track = all`, with `nested_tags = inherit` or `scan`) |
| `inlist-simple-cN` | a 10,000-element `IN` list (59 kB) with the comment at the end; `inlist` ends with `;` (the server gets the statement's length), `inlist0` does not (`stmt_len = 0`, so a `strlen` finds the end) | `inlist-pgss`, `inlist-ext` (`position = append`: heuristic tail scan), `inlist-ext-any` (`position = any`: a full exact scan); and the same three for `inlist0` |
| `evict-simple-cN` | the `ro` statement, but with a random `controller` value: a new tag set on almost every transaction, so the store evicts continuously | `pgss`; `ext-max10000` (`max_entries = 10000`) |
| `ro-simple-c256`, `rw-simple-c256` | opt-in (`--high-clients`) | `pgss`; `ext` |

Every run is checked, and the run fails if a check fails: the preload list, that pgss and this extension counted every statement (and the extension recorded the expected tags, including the normalized `route` and the regex's `driver`), that nested statements were attributed as configured, which scan path the IN lists took (`_info().heuristic_scans`), that eviction and bucket rollovers happened, that the store held 5,000 extra entries, and that the reader read rows. The **Validation** table of each campaign shows the values.

## Method

- **One container, Unix socket.** The server and pgbench run in one `docker run --rm` container and talk over the Unix socket. The server is pinned with `taskset` to the first half of the container's CPUs and pgbench to the next 30%. The rest stay idle for the OS and the reader.
- **Release build.** A plain `make`, without `PSSC_TESTING`, as packaged.
- **Server.** `shared_buffers = 1GB`, `autovacuum = off`, `jit = off`, `synchronous_commit = off`, `max_wal_size = 8GB`, `checkpoint_timeout = 30min`, pgbench scale 10 (prewarmed with `pg_prewarm`). Both extensions use their defaults unless a configuration says otherwise (`pg_stat_statements.max = 5000`, `track = top`; this extension: `extractors = 'sqlcommenter, marginalia'`, `tags = action, controller, job`, `scan_window = 2kB`, `max_entries = 10000`, 12 × 5 min buckets). `env-container.txt` in each results directory lists every setting.
- **One run.** Restart the server, prewarm, (for `rw`) truncate `pgbench_history` and `VACUUM`, warm up with the same script, reset both extensions' statistics, then run pgbench for the measured duration with `--log`.
- **Blocks.** A block runs every configuration once. Within a block, the scenarios come in a random order, and so do the configurations within a scenario; the seed is recorded. A configuration and its baseline from the same block form a **pair**, so slow drift over the campaign affects both sides of a pair alike, and the random order keeps a fixed position from favoring either side.
- **Server CPU per statement.** The CPU time (user + system) of the postmaster and all its children during the measured run, from `/proc/<pid>/stat` (including the children that exited, through the postmaster's `cutime`/`cstime`), divided by the transactions, and by the statements per transaction (1 for `ro`, 7 for `rw` including `BEGIN`/`END`, 17 for `nested`). This is the most direct measure of what the extension adds: it counts the work and ignores the time a backend waits. It includes pgss's and PostgreSQL's own work, and the reader's queries in the reader configurations.
- **Latency.** p50, p95 and p99 of transaction latency, nearest-rank over every transaction of the run, from pgbench's per-transaction log. Only the per-run summaries are kept, not the logs.
- **Statistics.** For each pair, the difference (configuration − baseline) of the CPU per statement, and the log ratio of TPS and of each latency percentile. The tables show the **mean over the pairs and its 95% confidence interval** (Student t with n − 1 degrees of freedom); ratios are shown as percentages (exp(mean log ratio) − 1). The verdict says *costlier* or *cheaper* when the interval excludes zero, and *not resolved* when it includes zero. Then its upper end is the largest overhead the data is consistent with, not a measurement of it. These intervals assume the pairs are independent and roughly normal. With a few pairs, one bad block widens them a lot; the raw per-run values are in `runs.jsonl`. The tables also mark (†) the differences that survive a Bonferroni correction over the whole table.
- **Interference.** The container sees the VM-wide `/proc/stat`. For each run, the CPU time used outside the container (VM busy time minus the container cgroup's usage) is recorded. A run where it exceeds 0.75 cores is repeated, up to 3 attempts. Before the campaign, `docker ps` must show no other containers; `campaign.json` records the check. Pauses of the macOS host or of the VM are not visible from inside and remain as noise.
- **Bucket boundaries.** Buckets start at multiples of the interval since 2000-01-01, so with 1 s buckets every whole second is a boundary. A transaction is *near* a boundary when its execution overlaps ±5 ms of a whole second. Every configuration is analyzed on the same grid; the ones without 1 s buckets are controls.

### What the data can and cannot bound

- A *costlier* or *cheaper* verdict means the effect is real on this host, within its interval. A *not resolved* verdict bounds the effect only by the interval's upper end, on this host.
- Nothing here bounds the overhead on other hardware: a different CPU, cache size, memory latency or kernel changes both the absolute costs and the ratio. The CPU per statement is the more portable number, as it does not depend on how saturated the server is.
- The `ro` statements are cheap, cached point selects: the worst case for relative overhead. Statements that do real work see proportionally less.
- **How to read an interval.** Each interval is computed from the n paired differences and assumes the pairs are independent and their differences (or log ratios) roughly normal. Under those assumptions, an interval that excludes zero is evidence of an effect even when the effect is much smaller than the run-to-run spread of the baseline (the *TPS spread* column): drift between blocks hits both sides of a pair and cancels in the difference. That is the point of pairing, so the spread is not a threshold.
- **VM noise.** Pauses of the macOS host, vCPU migration between performance and efficiency cores, and the VM's own housekeeping hit either side of a pair at random; with the randomized order they widen the intervals rather than shift them. They do make the differences of p95 and p99 heavy-tailed, so the t interval is only approximate there, and one bad block can move it. Pairing also does not remove every systematic difference: `inlist-ext` came out about 5% *cheaper* than its baseline, which no mechanism explains. Effects of a few percent from this host deserve confirmation on dedicated hardware.
- **Pointwise versus simultaneous.** Each interval is a pointwise 95% interval: across many comparisons, about 5% of them exclude zero by chance even when nothing changed. The latency table states how many of its intervals exclude zero against that expectation. Its † marks the intervals that still exclude zero when all of the table's intervals are made simultaneous (Bonferroni: each at 1 − 0.05/M); those are unlikely to be chance findings of the whole table. An interval without † that excludes zero is still the best estimate of its effect, but a single one of them could be a chance finding, so weigh it with related results (for example, the CPU cost of the same configuration).
- Not measured: `untagged = record`, `prepend` mode, the `marginalia` and `appname` extractors on their own, `cardinality_cap` (see the microbenchmark below), exemplars, `reclaim_worker`, very large `max_entries`, network latency between client and server, and PostgreSQL majors other than the one each campaign names (run `bench/run.sh --major N` for those).

## Long statements: scan costs and tuning

The extension finds comments by lexing the statement's text (no extra parse). For a statement of *L* bytes, with the default `scan_window = 2kB`:

| Case | Bytes examined | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| *L* ≤ `scan_window` | all *L*, once | always exact |
| `position = prepend` | the first `scan_window` | exact |
| `position = append` (default), the statement ends with `;` or is one of several in a string | the last `scan_window` | heuristic: walks back from the last `*/` (counted in `_info().heuristic_scans`; see [Where comments are found](extractors.md#where-comments-are-found)) |
| `position = append`, no trailing `;` | the last `scan_window`, **after a `strlen` of the statement** | the server reports `stmt_len = 0` ("to the end of the string"), so the end is found with `strlen`, which reads all *L* bytes. `strlen` is fast (vectorized), so this is small next to parsing |
| `position = any` | all *L* bytes, lexed one by one | exact, and grows linearly with *L* |
| a larger `scan_window` | up to `scan_window` bytes | a window ≥ *L* makes the scan exact, at the cost of `any` |

The `inlist` scenarios measure these cases on a 59 kB statement. Parsing and planning a 10,000-element `IN` list costs about 6 ms of CPU in PostgreSQL itself, so the extension's share is small even with `any`. The local campaign could not resolve it: `any` measured +0.2 ms [−0.6, +1.0] with a trailing `;` and +0.3 ms [−0.2, +0.8] without, against a residual bias of about 5% between runs, so a full scan of 59 kB costs less than about 1 ms there. The heuristic `append` path, with or without the `strlen`, reads at most 2 kB plus the length scan and was not distinguishable from zero.

Tuning:

- Keep the default `position = append` (or use `prepend` if your driver prepends comments) for workloads with long statements. Use `position = any` only if comments can appear in the middle of statements.
- Leave `scan_window` at 2 kB unless comments are longer, or sit further from the end, than that. Raising it to cover whole long statements buys exactness at the cost of `any`.
- If `_info().heuristic_scans` grows and you want exact attribution of long statements, compare the cost of `any` below with your statements' execution time before switching.
- There is no separate byte budget: `scan_window` is the knob.

The same guidance is in [configuration.md](configuration.md#scan_window).

## Microbenchmarks of single features

These were measured with test-only SQL functions (`make PSSC_TESTING=1`) when the features were added, and have not been re-run since. They resolve costs of a few nanoseconds, which pgbench cannot.

### Activity view

Measured at commit `23d9382` (PG 18.6, Docker on the same M1, `max_connections = 100`, so 136 slots). The [activity view](sql-interface.md#pg_stat_statement_context_activity) adds a lock-free write to the backend's shared slot at the start and at the end of each top-level statement. `pssc_context_test_activity_bench(loops, tags_bytes)` (in `test/modules/pssc_context_test`) times one publish plus one set-idle, the pair a statement causes, and a read of all slots:

| Tag set size | Writer, per pair | Reader, all 136 slots |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 B | 5.6 ns | ~0.5–0.6 µs |
| 30 B (typical) | 5.9 ns | |
| 190 B | 44 ns | |
| 512 B (`max_tagset_bytes`) | 110 ns | |

A `SELECT` publishes at `ExecutorRun` and again at `ExecutorFinish`, so it makes two pairs: about 12 ns per statement with typical tags. Reading the view from SQL (one client, 9 backends): `SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_statement_context_activity` takes 0.077 ms, `pg_stat_activity` 0.289 ms, and the join of both 0.355 ms.

### Cardinality caps

Measured at commit `002dda0` (PG 18.6, Docker on the same M1). With a [cardinality cap](configuration.md#cardinality_cap), each kept tag costs a lookup in a shared, lock-free hash table. 200,000 calls of [`pg_stat_statement_context_extract()`](sql-interface.md#pg_stat_statement_context_extract) on `SELECT 1 /*action='show',controller='users'*/` (2 kept tags) in a PL/pgSQL loop; median of 5 alternating rounds per case:

| Case | ns per call | vs caps off |
|---|---:|---:|
| caps off (`cardinality_cap = 0`: no check at all) | 3244 | |
| both values admitted (steady state) | 3305 | +61 ns (≈ 30 ns per tag) |
| `controller` over its cap (collapses to `null`) | 3282 | +38 ns |
| table full (`cardinality_cap_slots = 256`, 64 probes per tag) | 3463 | +144 ns (vs 3319 off in the same run) |

Earlier pgbench A/B runs of both features, in the previous version of this page, were too noisy to resolve these costs; they are superseded by the campaigns below.

